Right now, the 3D assets appear dark in the Simulator but normal in the Preview.

I wanted to implement a swipe right / swipe left feature where the user can look at the product and wave with an open hand to navigate to the next product. It would work like Tinder, where a swipe right would indicate that they liked the product while a swipe left would indicate that they didn't like the product. This would help train the recommender system (which I will refer to as the 'RecSys' from now on). 

However, I want to make sure that the swipe gestures don't interfere with purchasing decisions. For example, if it is more comfortable for a certain user to swipe left than to swipe right, the user may be inclined to swipe left more often. 

And, if the user can easily navigate to the next product with the wave of a hand, it may result in a circumstance similar to endless scrolling, where the user enters a "trance" of swiping through products. If this happens, then they would most likely be swiping in the same direction every time, which would cause the RecSys to misrepresent the user's tastes and preferences. Therefore, I am rethinking the idea of swiping left or right to navigate to the next product and indicate preferences.

Also, I think I might need a more intuitive gesture for repositioning the asset in 3D space. Currently, there is a button in the bottom-right corner of the window (which I will refer to as the "Cube Button" from now on). This button was meant to be pinched and held to allow the user to then drag the 3D model around in 3D space. However, I think the user should instead be able to pinch and hold on the product itself (with one hand) to drag it around in 3D space. I was originally planning to allow the user to rotate the product by pinching with one hand, but I will now use the swipe left and swipe right gestures (with an open hand) for rotating the object (either clockwise or counter-clockwise, accordingly) in its current location. Remember, those swiping gestures were originally used to navigate to another product but now they should be used for rotating the 3D asset in place. These changes also free up the Cube Button to serve as a button that can transition the user into a more immersive space with the product. 

But, how do I let users easily provide feedback to train the RecSys? (Now that it's no longer being done with swiping gestures). 

I guess there are enough opportunities for the RecSys to gather feedback from the user's actions without specific "Like or Disklike" indicators from the user. For example, if the user adds to cart, saves, creates a collection with, or buys the product, then those can indicate "Like"'s (with different weights) for the RecSys. Similarly, if the user navigates to the next product without interacting with it at all, then that can indicate a "Disklike" for the RecSys. Plus, the user should be able to give feedback vocally in natural language, which will be interpreted and turned into training data. But I don't think the user should be able to navigate to the next product with a simple voice command. For example, this might lead some users to say, "No. No. No. No. ..." as they swipe through products, which could make bystanders dislike the app, its culture, and the concept of 3D shopping as a whole. In fact, the natural language interface should be COMPLETELY optional so that people can shop silently with full functionality if they so please.


There should also be a cool hand gesture that adds a product to cart (for example, pointing at it like: 👉).




